WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.709
The Avonlea Improvement Society met at Green Gables that evening.

2
00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:08.496
The members had important business to discuss.

3
00:00:08.497 --> 00:00:13.060
The society was flourishing and had already accomplished wonders.

4
00:00:13.061 --> 00:00:17.262
Local farmers had painted their fences and cleaned up the roadsides.

5
00:00:17.263 --> 00:00:25.082
No one objected when the society planted a new garden of geraniums at the crossroads, where two main roads met.

6
00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:31.284
The meeting was about to start when Gertie Pye arrived. Gertie was often late.

7
00:00:31.285 --> 00:00:36.077
Some people said she did it on purpose—to make her entrance more dramatic.

8
00:00:36.078 --> 00:00:42.165
Gertie stood in the middle of the room and exclaimed, "I've just heard something awful.

9
00:00:42.166 --> 00:00:50.082
Mr. Parker is going to rent all of his fences to a medicine company for advertising!"

10
00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:53.079
"It can't be true," said Anne blankly.

11
00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:57.632
"That's just what I said," said Gertie, enjoying the attention.

12
00:00:57.633 --> 00:01:00.006
"His farm is on the main road.

13
00:01:00.007 --> 00:01:05.833
It'll be awful to see advertisements for pills all along his fence!"

14
00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:13.162
Everybody talked at once, saying how ugly the main road of Avonlea would look with the advertisements.

15
00:01:13.163 --> 00:01:19.562
"Stay calm," implored Anne, "and let's try to think of some way to stop him."

16
00:01:19.563 --> 00:01:23.229
"How can we stop him?" asked Jane bitterly.

17
00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:30.473
"Everybody knows Sam Parker would do anything for money. He doesn't care about the community."

18
00:01:30.474 --> 00:01:38.364
The situation did not look promising. After much discussion Anne agreed to talk to Mr. Parker.

19
00:01:38.365 --> 00:01:44.212
Anne met with Mr. Parker the next day, but Mr. Parker refused to change his mind.

20
00:01:44.213 --> 00:01:54.858
"However," he said smiling, "I'll tell the painters to use only pretty colors when they paint the advertisements. They mustn't use blue

21
00:01:54.859 --> 00:01:59.913
!" Mr. Parker was, of course, referring to the incident of the blue town

22
00:01:59.914 --> 00:02:04.683
hall. Anne knew when she was being made fun of, and she said

23
00:02:04.684 --> 00:02:12.041
good-bye. The next day two men, Mr. Parker and Jerry Walker, were sitting in their buggies at the side of the

24
00:02:12.042 --> 00:02:18.919
road. Canada was on the eve of a general election, and Mr. Walker was busy campaigning for his party's

25
00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:26.346
candidate. Anne was walking quietly to the graveyard, as she did every Saturday, to lay flowers on Matthew Cuthbert's

26
00:02:26.347 --> 00:02:31.990
grave. Anne had loved Marilla's kind brother, Matthew, and she missed him every

27
00:02:31.991 --> 00:02:40.829
day. As Anne came around the corner, she heard Mr. Walker say, "If you'll vote for him, I'll give you one-hundred

28
00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:45.343
dollars." "Well," said Mr. Parker with a grin, "all

29
00:02:45.344 --> 00:02:50.597
right." At this moment, both men saw Anne, and their conversation abruptly

30
00:02:50.598 --> 00:02:54.584
ceased. Anne looked at them frostily and walked on.

31
00:02:54.585 --> 00:02:59.994
Mr. Parker rode up on his buggy and inquired, "Would you like a lift, Anne?"

32
00:02:59.995 --> 00:03:03.667
"No, thank you," said Anne politely.

33
00:03:03.668 --> 00:03:09.219
Mr. Parker usually didn't care what people thought of him, but this situation was different.

34
00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:13.156
He didn't want people to know he had accepted a bribe.

35
00:03:13.157 --> 00:03:21.541
"Excuse me," he said, hesitating. "Anne, I've decided not to rent my fences for advertisements.

36
00:03:21.542 --> 00:03:29.360
And I . . . um . . . hope you won't mention that little conversation with Mr. Walker to anyone."

37
00:03:29.361 --> 00:03:32.900
"No, I won't," said Anne icily.

38
00:03:32.901 --> 00:03:38.805
Anne didn't gossip and would never discuss a private conversation she had overheard.

39
00:03:38.806 --> 00:03:45.748
But Mr. Parker thought she was making a deal, and so he never did rent his fences for advertisements.

40
00:03:45.749 --> 00:03:50.523
Quite by luck the Improvement Society's problem was solved.

41
00:03:50.524 --> 00:03:55.623
As Anne walked on, she spotted a small boy sitting at the crossroads.

42
00:03:55.624 --> 00:03:58.843
The boy had big eyes and a sensitive face.

43
00:03:58.844 --> 00:04:02.967
It was Paul Irving—one of Anne's favorite students.

44
00:04:02.968 --> 00:04:11.737
Paul stood up and smiled. "I waited for you, Teacher, because I knew you were going to the graveyard." He put his hand in hers.

45
00:04:11.738 --> 00:04:16.102
In his other hand, he carried a bunch of fresh white roses.

46
00:04:16.103 --> 00:04:20.236
"I'm going there, too, to put these roses on my mother's grave.

47
00:04:20.237 --> 00:04:25.196
You see, Teacher, today is exactly three years since my mother died.

48
00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:30.645
It's such a long, long time, but when I think about her it still hurts just as much . . ."

49
00:04:30.646 --> 00:04:33.873
Paul's voice quivered and his lip trembled.

50
00:04:33.874 --> 00:04:38.719
"Sometimes I can't bear it, it hurts so."

51
00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:44.537
"And yet," said Anne, very softly, "you wouldn't want it to stop hurting, would you?

52
00:04:44.538 --> 00:04:47.888
You wouldn't want to forget your mother even if you could."

53
00:04:47.889 --> 00:04:55.392
"No, I wouldn't. That's just the way I feel," Paul said. "You're so good at understanding, Teacher."

54
00:04:55.393 --> 00:05:02.796
When they reached the cemetery, Paul said, "Father understood pretty well, but still I couldn't talk to him about Mother much.

55
00:05:02.797 --> 00:05:09.699
It made him feel so sad." He gently laid the roses down on his mother's grave.

56
00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:14.302
"Poor Father, he must be dreadfully lonely without me."

57
00:05:14.303 --> 00:05:21.711
Anne thought sadly of her own mother and father, who had both died so long ago, when Anne was just a baby.

58
00:05:21.712 --> 00:05:27.850
Anne could not remember her mother or father, and for this reason she almost envied Paul.

59
00:05:27.851 --> 00:05:40.307
She put flowers on Matthew's grave and walked home with Paul in the soft evening light.

